# OmniStudio deploys = mandatory cache-bust dance

## ⛔ Flavour gate — run this FIRST, every time

This rule describes **standard OmniStudio**. On a **Vlocity CMT** org none of it applies: the `OmniProcess` family is empty by design, so every command below succeeds while deleting nothing, and the self-check at the bottom ("0 compilation rows") passes vacuously. You would conclude the cache was busted when nothing was touched.

```bash
sf data query -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} -q "SELECT COUNT() FROM OmniProcess" --json | jq '.result.totalSize'
```

- **Greater than 0** → standard OmniStudio. Continue with this rule.
- **0** → **STOP.** Confirm with `sf package installed list -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --json | jq -r '.result[].SubscriberPackageNamespace' | grep -qx vlocity_cmt`. This org stores OmniStudio in `vlocity_cmt__OmniScript__c` / `vlocity_cmt__Element__c` / `vlocity_cmt__DRBundle__c`. Invalidate via `vlocity packDeploy` plus re-activation in OmniStudio Designer — not via anything below.

**OmniStudio runs from compiled artifacts cached in `OmniProcessCompilation` rows, not from the metadata you just deployed.** A vanilla `sf project deploy start` doesn't reliably invalidate that cache. **Either delete the compilation row OR run the full 5-step deactivate/reactivate dance** before assuming your deploy took effect. Apply this for **every deploy of an active OmniProcess version** — not "when symptoms appear" (by the time probes don't fire you've already burned an hour debugging working code).

> Retrieve before editing ([`retrieve-before-edit.mdc`](./retrieve-before-edit.mdc)) — OmniStudio metadata drifts especially fast because admins edit IPs/DRs directly in the org UI. Pass implementation Gate B from [`adversarial-review.mdc`](./adversarial-review.mdc) before deleting compilations, swapping active versions, or deploying. Companion skill: [`.claude/skills/omnistudio-deploy-cache-bust/SKILL.md`](../../.claude/skills/omnistudio-deploy-cache-bust/SKILL.md) — keep both in sync.
> Every anonymous-Apex operation below uses the TraceFlag/time-window/user/job correlation and async polling from [`apex-development.mdc`](./apex-development.mdc); `sf apex list log` alone is not proof of lineage.

## The cache model (the WHY)

- Every OmniScript / IP / FlexCard is one **`OmniProcess`** row (`UniqueName = <Type>_<SubType>_<Lang>_<Version>`, or `<Type>_<SubType>_Procedure_<Version>` for IPs); its steps are **`OmniProcessElement`** rows.
- On activation (`IsActive: false → true`) the package compiles the element tree into one **`OmniProcessCompilation`** row (`OmniProcessId = <OmniProcess.Id>`). The runtime reads THAT, not the metadata. Until it's invalidated, your edits are invisible.
- IPs also have an **`OmniIntegrationProcConfig`** row (`DeveloperName = <UniqueName>`) that callers reference — this is why deleting an actively-called IP throws `DEPENDENCY_EXISTS`.
- **DataRaptors live in `OmniDataTransform`, are interpreted, and have NO compilation cache — skip this entire rule for `*.rpt-meta.xml`.** (Caveat: a DR change can still look invisible if its *caller* is stale-cached — bust the caller.)

> 🚨 **Caller-side cache — the trap that catches everyone.** When OmniProcess A calls B (a "Run Integration Procedure" step), A's compilation embeds a snapshot of B's compiled artifact. Busting B alone is NOT enough — A keeps serving the old B. **Bust the entire caller chain from the entry point down to your edited component** (§ Caller chain below).

## When to apply / skip

**Apply** when: deploying a manifest containing `OmniScript`/`OmniIntegrationProcedure`/any `Omni*`; you edited a file under `omniScripts/`, `omniIntegrationProcedures/`, or a FlexCard; adding probes; changing a conditional formula, input mapping, response JSON path, or remote-action class on an active version; renaming/reordering elements; swapping a DR/Apex/IP bundle; or migrating where the component already exists in the target.

**Skip** when: deploying a brand-new version that never existed (first creation always recompiles); a version whose file is `<isActive>false</isActive>`; pure DataRaptor changes; pure CustomField/ValidationRule/Layout/Apex changes touching no Omni XML; fresh scratch/sandbox bootstrap.

**Symptoms the cache is stale** (run the dance, don't debug your code): a just-added probe/Set-Values/Remote-Action isn't in the IP debug response; a conditional fires/skips against your edited `executionConditionalFormula`; a DR call uses the old `bundle`; a renamed step still shows the old name; two identical-input runs differ; description tweak deploys as `Unchanged`.

## Preparation — final bytes BEFORE Gate B

1. Apply every functional edit **and** the one-character top-level `<description>` byte-flip before snapshot/review.
2. Dry-run the final manifest and survey the exact Type/SubType/Language family plus caller-chain process IDs.
3. Before Gate B, create exact executable invalidation/reactivation scripts and compensating metadata rollback under `.sf-ops/<timestamp>/` (gitignored). Paste each script into the change doc's diff-highlights so Gate B reviews the exact bytes you will run.
4. Run Gate B against final bytes, caller-chain IDs, dry-run/runtime evidence, operation scripts, and rollback artifact.
5. Re-check component freshness immediately before deploying, then run deploy and invalidation as one uninterrupted sequence — the shorter that window, the smaller the race. If anyone else deploys into the family mid-sequence, abort and rerun Gate B. Any byte, caller-chain, operation-script, or org-state change also reruns Gate B.

Never edit `<description>` or any metadata after Gate B.

## Path A — deploy first, then invalidate exact compilations

Use for a low-risk edit when same-version reactivation is unnecessary.

```bash
# 1. Deploy the already-reviewed final bytes.
sf project deploy start --manifest manifest/<feature>.xml -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --ignore-conflicts --wait 5

# 2. AFTER deploy, delete exact Vcurrent + reviewed caller-chain compilations atomically.
cat > .sf-ops/<timestamp>/invalidate-reviewed-chain.apex <<'EOF'
delete [SELECT Id FROM OmniProcessCompilation WHERE OmniProcessId IN ('<current>','<caller-1>','<caller-2>')];
EOF
sf apex run -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --file .sf-ops/<timestamp>/invalidate-reviewed-chain.apex
sf apex list log -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --json
```

Deploy-first prevents an invocation from recompiling old metadata between invalidation and deployment. Run the self-check; if runtime still serves stale logic, use Path B.

## Path B — atomic same-version reactivation

Use one Apex transaction after deploying reviewed bytes. If any statement fails, Salesforce rolls back the whole transaction, so the process remains active—no `Vold` swap or downtime recovery is needed.

```bash
# 1. Deploy reviewed final bytes first.
sf project deploy start --manifest manifest/<feature>.xml -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --ignore-conflicts --wait 5

# 2. Prepare one atomic reactivation transaction with exact reviewed IDs.
cat > .sf-ops/<timestamp>/reactivate-current.apex <<'EOF'
Set<Id> reviewedProcessIds = new Set<Id>{'<Vcurrent.Id>','<caller-1>','<caller-2>'};
List<OmniProcess> lockedProcesses = [SELECT Id, IsActive FROM OmniProcess WHERE Id IN :reviewedProcessIds ORDER BY Id FOR UPDATE];
if (lockedProcesses.size() != reviewedProcessIds.size()) throw new IllegalArgumentException('Reviewed process set changed');
OmniProcess current;
for (OmniProcess process : lockedProcesses) if (process.Id == '<Vcurrent.Id>') current = process;
if (!current.IsActive) throw new IllegalArgumentException('Expected Vcurrent active');
update new OmniProcess(Id = current.Id, IsActive = false);
delete [SELECT Id FROM OmniProcessCompilation WHERE OmniProcessId IN :reviewedProcessIds];
update new OmniProcess(Id = current.Id, IsActive = true);
EOF
sf apex run -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --file .sf-ops/<timestamp>/reactivate-current.apex
sf apex list log -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --json
# Fetch/inspect every log ID created by this anonymous-Apex operation.
```

Do NOT delete `OmniIntegrationProcConfig`, any `OmniProcess`, or local metadata files. A concurrent operator either waits on `FOR UPDATE` or fails/rolls back; it cannot commit a half-active family. If post-deploy verification fails, execute the exact Gate-B-reviewed compensating metadata deploy, verify prior runtime behavior/compilation, then rerun Gate B against the new revision before any forward retry.

## Caller chain — the #1 reason "the dance worked but probes still don't fire"

Editing a callee does NOT invalidate caller compilations — there's no automatic cascade. Bust every OmniProcess from the entry point down to your edit (Path A compilation-delete on each):

```bash
# Find IPs whose elements reference your edited IP
sf data query -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --query "SELECT OmniProcess.UniqueName, Name FROM OmniProcessElement WHERE PropertySetConfig LIKE '%<callee_short_name>%' AND Type = 'Integration Procedure Action' AND OmniProcess.IsActive = true"
# Then delete all their compilations in one apex run:
cat > /tmp/nuke-chain.apex <<'EOF'
delete [SELECT Id FROM OmniProcessCompilation WHERE OmniProcessId IN ('<entry>','<mid>','<edited>')];
EOF
sf apex run -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --file /tmp/nuke-chain.apex
sf apex list log -o {{ORG_ALIAS}} --json
# Fetch/inspect every log ID created by this anonymous-Apex operation.
```

Diagnostic: if the parent IP's `OmniProcessCompilation.LastModifiedDate` predates your edit's activation, the parent is serving stale — that's the culprit. Never “nuke all stale compilations” by default: any bulk deletion outside the proven caller chain requires an explicit adversarial finding/disposition, exact scoped IDs, user approval, and a recovery plan because unrelated active features pay the recompile/concurrency cost. For a multi-component feature, bust **leaves first** (callee → callers → top OmniScript) or do one reviewed, ID-scoped compilation-delete + one bulk redeploy.

## Edge cases

- **Dry-run/deploy reports `Unchanged`:** the pre-Gate-B description flip didn't persist. Stop before mutation, fix it, then rerun the gate's critics against the new revision.
- **OmniScripts add a browser cache layer:** after the server-side bust, hard-refresh (Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+R) the host Lightning page, or sign out/in.

## Self-check (before declaring success)

- [ ] Flavour gate ran and returned a non-zero `OmniProcess` count — otherwise every check below is vacuous.
- [ ] `OmniProcess`: Vcurrent `IsActive=true`, `LastModifiedDate` within the last minute.
- [ ] `OmniProcessElement`: your changed elements have NEW `Id`s.
- [ ] Before first invocation: 0 compilation rows; OR after a concurrent/intentional invocation: compilation `LastModifiedDate` is post-deploy and runtime behavior/probes prove it compiled the reviewed elements.
- [ ] If probes write to a sink, trigger the flow and confirm the probe label appears.
- [ ] `git diff` shows only your intended change + the description byte-flip.
- [ ] Gate B reviewer identities, reviewed diff, caller-chain scope, findings/dispositions, and re-review verdict are recorded.

## Common errors (literal strings)

| Error / symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `FIELD_INTEGRITY_EXCEPTION: You can't update or delete an active Omniscript record...` | Attempted process/config deletion or unsupported active-state mutation | Use reviewed Path A or atomic Path B; never delete process/config rows |
| `UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW` | Another operator owns the family/current row | Abort; recheck org state, then rerun Gate B against the new revision |
| `DEPENDENCY_EXISTS: ... Omni Integration Procedure Configuration` | Someone attempted unnecessary config/process deletion | Keep config/process rows; delete only the compilation |
| `Status: Succeeded` + `State: Unchanged` | File matches org byte-for-byte | Description tweak didn't persist — re-edit |
| `No source-backed components present...` | Manifest member used slash syntax | Use `<Type>_<SubType>_Procedure_<N>` underscores |
| `sObject type 'OmniProcess' is not supported.` | Used `--use-tooling-api` | Drop it (standard data API) |
| Probe never appears in its sink | Cache not busted, or wrong block | Add an unconditional top-level probe; if even that fails, it's cache |

## Why it works

The runtime caches the deserialized `OmniProcessCompilation` payload keyed on Type/SubType. Both paths deploy reviewed bytes first. Path A then removes exact stale artifacts so the next invocation compiles deployed metadata. Path B additionally performs false→true reactivation and exact compilation deletion in one rollback-safe transaction. The pre-review description byte-flip forces MDAPI to emit `Changed` instead of short-circuiting on `Unchanged`.
